you just stated it correctly we call it a Community cat program or more specifically a community cat initiative because it's a suite of programming um those kind of complaints usually spur from you know the reality of the overpopulation of free roaming cats in our community um and this is It's a universal problem any place that we have human beings um existing in society together we also have free roaming domestic C populations um and this has been going on for decades and decades and it used to be that the model to approach this sort of nuisance cat population was to round them up and kill them and what we have found through Decades of scientific research is that that is completely ineffective in managing those populations in the longterm because of cat behavior so

domestic cats are highly territorial species and they tend to form really strong social bonds and so what we know is if we take an individual out of an area we create a vacuum other cats move into that area and then they're not run out because the territorial cat that was there before doesn't no longer exists in order to keep those others sort of Interlopers at Bay um so through you know Decades of trial and error and lots and lots of field research and we have plenty of peer review data to support this we know now that the best most scientifically sound way to reduce free roaming cap populations over time is through what we call these TNR or RTF programs which means that cats that are brought we go into areas where there are free roaming cats and

we spay and neuter them and we vaccinate them particularly against rabies to protect against zoonotic disease but we also vaccinate them for common feline specific diseases but then we leave the cats there because what happens is if we take the cats out a there's often no other place for them to go um and and animal shelters are just no longer willing to be a place that animals go to be exterminated um and B we just continue to perpetuate that population growth because the animals that remain will uh reproduce faster in light of that decreased that temporary decrease in population and also because there are clearly already resources there to support cats other cats will move into the area um

so what we're doing is effectively limiting the opportunity for population growth by spaying and neutering those cats but also we're putting them back where they where they came from so that we can prevent new cats who are not spayed and neutered from moving into that area and when we do that in a targeted way over a period of time which admittedly we're not doing in this community because we don't have the funding for it um but that will effectively reduce that population growth over a period of time so I think it's actually programming that should be reinvested and we should be putting more resources toward it if we have concerns from constituents and the public about these C populations um

but this is this is the best tool that we have at our disposal I would even point out that before my tenure here the community cat initiative that was put into city code even says it is the best known tool that we have for managing these populations so that's that's kind of what I tell people you know the other thing is most free roaming cats are owned by somebody so animal control does not pick them up this is an agreement that we have with the city to partner on this initiative they don't bring in Stray Cats unless those cats are obviously ill or injured it's the public that traps them and brings them in or the cranky neighbor that picks it up and

